TOPOLOGY AS TESTED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The NTP client is the testbox itself. It sends the request and reads the reply.

    tb277 eth3 10.38.22.33/27                 tb277 eth4 10.38.22.65/27
    (NTP client leg A + capture)              (NTP client leg B)
         |      ^                                  |      ^
   NTP   |      |  NTP reply                 NTP   |      |  NTP reply
 request |      |  (mode 4)                request |      |  (mode 4)
         v      |                                  v      |
  +--------------------------------------------------------------------+
  |  swi_d   AT-x960-56YSQ          *** DUT -- the NTP server ***       |
  |  port1.0.10, access, vlan10 10.38.22.34/27                          |
  |  port1.0.48, access, vlan48 10.38.22.66/27                          |
  +--------------------------------------------------------------------+

  DUT      : swi_d (AT-x960-56YSQ) console u2 @115200, S/N A2WQS700B,
             software sin_x96056-20261019-27. Not a stack: one unit.
  client   : tb277 itself. It is the NTP client for both legs and also the capture point.
  link A   : tb277 eth3 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e 10.38.22.33/27 -> swi_d port1.0.10 (copper,
             access port in vlan10; the DUT's address on that VLAN is 10.38.22.34/27).
             Pinged 3 of 3, 0% loss, rtt avg 0.537 ms before step 1.
  link B   : tb277 eth4 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f 10.38.22.65/27 -> swi_d port1.0.48 (copper,
             access port in vlan48; the DUT's address on that VLAN is 10.38.22.66/27).
             Pinged 3 of 3, 0% loss, rtt avg 0.347 ms before step 1.
  tools    : tools/ntpc.py, a crafted NTP client over a plain UDP socket, run on tb277.
             It builds each 48-byte request itself, decodes every field of the reply, and
             reports origin_echo=yes when the reply echoes that request's transmit
             timestamp. Exit code 2 means no reply, which is a valid control result.
             tools/ntpwire.py, run on tb277 over each tcpdump capture. It counts NTP
             packets by mode and by address pair, and pairs each reply with its request.
             It is the independent witness: it proves a request left the box even when
             no reply came back.
  clocks   : swi_d and tb277 both run on UTC. Every time in this log is UTC.

STEP 1 -- control: with no NTP configuration, the DUT answers nothing on UDP/123    PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: send real NTP requests to the DUT before any NTP configuration exists, and
  capture them at the same time. This proves that every reply counted later is caused by
  the configuration under test, and not by something that was already answering.

awplus#show running-config | include ntp
  (nothing -- there is no NTP configuration of any kind on this DUT)

tb277$ tools/tb capture tb277 ntp1021 eth3 16 t33399-s1-off.pcap udp port 123   (background)
tb277$ tools/tb tool tb277 ntp1021 ntpc 10.38.22.34 --bind 10.38.22.33 --count 3 --gap 2 --timeout 3
ntpc: IPv4  10.38.22.33:53176 -> 10.38.22.34:123   version=4 mode=3 count=3 gap=2.0s timeout=3.0s
  #1 sent 2026-10-21 03:31:12.447058Z -> NO REPLY within 3.0 s
  #2 sent 2026-10-21 03:31:17.453466Z -> NO REPLY within 3.0 s
  #3 sent 2026-10-21 03:31:22.460192Z -> NO REPLY within 3.0 s

SUMMARY: sent=3 replies=0 origin_echo=0 no_reply=3
                                           <-- exit code 2, the expected control result

  ntpwire on the capture (the capture window closed before packet #3, so it holds 2 of 3):
03:31:12.447149 v4  10.38.22.33:53176 -> 10.38.22.34:123  client v4 LI=ok st=0 refid=----
03:31:17.453631 v4  10.38.22.33:53176 -> 10.38.22.34:123  client v4 LI=ok st=0 refid=----
  by mode:   client(3)=2
  pairing:   requests=2 replies=0  paired=0 unpaired=0 unanswered=2
                                           <-- the requests were on the wire, and nothing came back
  => STEP 1 PASS: the requests really left the testbox, and the DUT sent nothing back.
     A `show` command cannot make that distinction, which is why the capture is run even
     on a leg where nothing is expected. The control is established.

