STEP 3 -- the DUT serves time: the exchange, field by field                        PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
tb277$ tools/tb capture tb277 ntp1021 eth3 25 t33399-s3-on.pcap udp port 123    (background)
tb277$ tools/tb tool tb277 ntp1021 ntpc 10.38.22.34 --bind 10.38.22.33 --count 3 --gap 2 --timeout 3
  #1 sent 2026-10-21 03:32:58.526595Z  reply 48 B from 10.38.22.34:123
      LI=0(no-warning) VN=4 mode=4(server) stratum=5 poll=4 precision=-25
      refid=127.127.1.1 (ascii '....')  root_delay=0.000000s root_dispersion=0.000000s
      reference=2026-10-21 03:31:59.885092Z
      origin   =2026-10-21 03:32:58.526531Z  origin_echo=yes
      receive  =2026-10-21 03:32:44.257702Z
      transmit =2026-10-21 03:32:44.257816Z
      offset=-14.269142 s  delay=0.000498 s   (server clock minus ours)
  ... #2 and #3 identical in every field except the timestamps

SUMMARY: sent=3 replies=3 origin_echo=3 no_reply=0
         reply stratum(s)=5 mode(s)=4
         offset min=-14.269191 max=-14.269128 mean=-14.269154 s

  Each field, and why it is the right value:

    field          measured                      why it is right
    -----          --------                      ----------------
    mode           4 (server)                    the DUT answered as a server, not as a peer
    LI             0, no warning                 it says it is synchronised, not LI=3
    stratum        5                             exactly the `ntp master 5` value, so the
                                                 configuration reached the packet
    refid          127.127.1.1                   the local-clock reference. It matches
                                                 `show ntp status`'s `Reference ID : 7F7F0101`
                                                 byte for byte (0x7F7F0101 = 127.127.1.1)
    origin         echoes our transmit,          the reply answers THIS request, not stray
                   origin_echo=yes on all 3      traffic
    root delay /   0 / 0                         correct for a stand-alone master whose
    dispersion                                   reference is itself
    offset         -14.269 s, spread of          the DUT's clock against the testbox clock,
                   63 microseconds over 3        now measured by NTP itself. Both ends run
                   samples                       on UTC; this is the real difference between
                                                 the two clocks

  The precision field disagrees between the CLI and the wire. `show ntp status` in step 2
  reported `Precision : 0 (1.000000000 seconds)`, and the packet served here carries
  `precision=-25`, which is about 30 nanoseconds. The two values are 25 binary orders of
  magnitude apart, so this is not a rounding difference. A client acts on the packet
  value, because the packet is all a client sees.

  Independent witness, ntpwire on the same capture:
  by mode:   client(3)=3  server(4)=3
  10.38.22.33 -> 10.38.22.34   3
  10.38.22.34 -> 10.38.22.33   3
  pairing:   requests=3 replies=3  paired=3 unpaired=0 unanswered=0
  => STEP 3 PASS: the DUT answered all 3 requests as an NTP server at the configured
     stratum 5, and it echoed each request's own transmit timestamp. The capture agrees
     with the client exactly: 3 requests, 3 replies, 3 paired, nothing unpaired. Two
     independent instruments give the same count, so the DUT is really serving time.

