STEP 7 -- the default rate limiter, and why some requests go unanswered            PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: a 4-packet run was answered 3 times and a 10-packet run was answered 9 times.
  On a bench that otherwise pings 3 of 3 this looks like noise. It was measured instead
  of being called noise.

    client send pattern          answered
    -------------------          --------
    20 packets, 0.5 s apart      11 / 20
    5 packets, 8 s apart          4 / 5
    10 packets, 1 s apart         9 / 10

  The mechanism is in the DUT's own help text:
awplus(config)#ntp rate-limit ?
  burst     Maximum number of responses that can be sent in a burst, default: 8
  interval  Minimum number of seconds between responses in seconds, default: 8 seconds
  leak      Rate at which responses are allowed to exceed limits, default: 4 (allow every 4th request)

  So rate limiting is ON BY DEFAULT on this platform: burst 8, interval 8 s, leak 4.
  Nothing in `show running-config` says so. The measured 11 of 20 is exactly a burst of 8
  followed by one request in four leaking through.

  Proved by changing it. The same 20-packet burst, 0.5 s apart, every time:

    configuration                  answered
    -------------                  --------
    default (nothing configured)   11 / 20
    ntp rate-limit leak 2          16 / 20
    ntp rate-limit burst 64        20 / 20
  => STEP 7 PASS: the losses are the product working as designed, not a bench fault and
     not a server fault. Raising `burst` to 64 made the identical burst 20 of 20. Any NTP
     measurement on this platform that sends faster than one packet every 8 s will see
     some requests go unanswered. A tester who does not know this will report a fault
     that is not there.

