tb277$ tools/tb tool tb277 ntp1021 ntpc 10.38.22.34 --bind 10.38.22.33 --count 4 --gap 2 --timeout 3
  #1 sent 2026-10-21 03:38:56.817055Z  reply 48 B from 10.38.22.34:123
      LI=0(no-warning) VN=4 mode=4(server) stratum=10 poll=4 precision=-25
      refid=127.127.1.1   origin_echo=yes
      offset=-14.835217 s  delay=0.001627 s
SUMMARY: sent=4 replies=3 origin_echo=3 no_reply=1
         reply stratum(s)=10 mode(s)=4
                                           <-- stratum 10 is the SAVED value, not a default

  ntpwire: requests=4 replies=3 paired=3 unpaired=0 unanswered=1
  The one unanswered request is step 7's default rate limiter, not a persistence failure:
  a 10-packet run in the same minute was answered 9 times, at the same stratum.

  Two measurements that came out of this step:
   - the DUT's clock survived the reboot, so the unit has a working real-time clock. It
     came back 14.835 s behind the testbox, against 14.269 s before the reboot. That is
     about 0.57 s of drift across one reboot, with no NTP client running to correct it.
   - `show reboot history` gained exactly one new row, `2026-10-21 03:37:28 Expected
     CLI(user request)`. That is this step's own reload, and it is matched.
  => STEP 8 PASS: the saved `ntp master 10` came back by itself after a cold boot. The DUT
     was serving time at the non-default stratum 10, on the wire, 13 s after the login
     prompt, before anyone logged in. This is reboot persistence proved as behaviour, not
     as a line in a configuration file.

